---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Faq from '../../../components/Faq.astro';

const schemas = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'How to Calculate Your TDEE, Step by Step, with Examples',
    description: 'Learn how to calculate TDEE by hand: BMR with Mifflin-St Jeor, the right activity multiplier, and goal targets, with full worked examples.',
    url: 'https://tdeecheck.com/blog/how-to-calculate-tdee/',
    author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Waqar', jobTitle: 'Dietitian' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TDEE Check', url: 'https://tdeecheck.com/' },
    datePublished: '2026-10-22',
    dateModified: '2026-10-22',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How do I calculate my TDEE manually?',
    a: 'First calculate BMR with Mifflin-St Jeor: (10 x weight in kg) + (6.25 x height in cm) minus (5 x age), then plus 5 for men or minus 161 for women. Then multiply by your activity factor: 1.2 sedentary, 1.375 light, 1.55 moderate, 1.725 very active, or 1.9 athlete. The result is your TDEE.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if I do not know my activity level?',
    a: 'Start with the level below the one you think you are. Most people overestimate. A desk worker training 3 days a week is usually light (1.375) or moderate (1.55). Track for 2 to 3 weeks and correct with real weight data.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I calculate TDEE for weight loss?',
    a: 'Calculate your normal TDEE first, then subtract 250 to 500 kcal. That is your weight loss intake target. Recalculate whenever your weight changes by about 10 to 15 lb, because a lighter body burns less.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Calculate Your TDEE, Step by Step (With Examples)"
  description="Learn how to calculate TDEE by hand: BMR with the Mifflin-St Jeor formula, picking the right activity multiplier, and setting goal calories. Two full worked examples."
  path="/blog/how-to-calculate-tdee/"
  ogType="article"
  schemas={schemas}
  crumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Blog', path: '/blog/' },
    { name: 'How to Calculate TDEE', path: '/blog/how-to-calculate-tdee/' },
  ]}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 pt-10">
    <p class="text-sm font-bold text-brand-700 uppercase tracking-wide">Guide · Reviewed by Waqar, Dietitian</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink-900">How to calculate your TDEE, step by step</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      You do not need an app to find your Total Daily Energy Expenditure. With four numbers about yourself and two short
      formulas you can work it out on paper in about two minutes. This guide walks through each step, then checks the math
      with two full examples. If you would rather skip the arithmetic, our
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/">TDEE Calculator</a> runs the identical steps instantly.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Step 1: Convert your weight and height</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      The formulas work in metric. If you think in pounds and inches, convert first. Weight in pounds divided by 2.205
      gives kilograms, so 165 lb becomes about 74.8 kg. Height in inches multiplied by 2.54 gives centimeters, so 5 ft
      6 in (66 inches) becomes about 167.6 cm. Write both numbers down.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Step 2: Calculate your BMR with Mifflin-St Jeor</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Your Basal Metabolic Rate is the calories you burn at complete rest. The Mifflin-St Jeor equation is:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl bg-brand-50 border border-brand-100 px-5 py-4 font-semibold text-ink-900">
      <p>Men: BMR = (10 x weight in kg) + (6.25 x height in cm) minus (5 x age) + 5</p>
      <p class="mt-2">Women: BMR = (10 x weight in kg) + (6.25 x height in cm) minus (5 x age) minus 161</p>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Do the multiplications first, then add and subtract left to right. Our
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/calculators/bmr-calculator/">BMR Calculator</a> shows this same
      step on its own if you want to double check your arithmetic.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Step 3: Multiply by your activity level</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      BMR assumes you never move. Real life costs more, so multiply by the factor that matches your whole week:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-brand-100 bg-white">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-brand-50 text-left">
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-extrabold text-ink-900">Lifestyle</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-extrabold text-ink-900">Multiply BMR by</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-brand-50">
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Sedentary, desk job, little exercise</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1.2</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Light, exercise 1 to 3 days a week</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1.375</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Moderate, exercise 3 to 5 days a week</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1.55</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Very active, hard exercise 6 to 7 days a week</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1.725</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Athlete, two sessions a day or physical job</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1.9</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Be honest here. Picking the flattering row is the most common reason calculated TDEE comes out too high, and it is
      better to start a touch low and adjust up than the reverse.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Worked example 1: a woman who wants to lose weight</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Sarah is 32, 5 ft 6 in, and 165 lb, and trains four days a week. Converting: 165 lb is 74.8 kg, and 66 inches is
      167.6 cm. Her BMR is (10 x 74.8) + (6.25 x 167.6) minus (5 x 32) minus 161, which is 748 + 1,048 minus 160 minus
      161, about <strong>1,475 kcal</strong>. Four training days makes her moderate, so her TDEE is 1,475 x 1.55, about
      <strong>2,287 kcal per day</strong>. For weight loss at roughly a pound a week she subtracts 500 and targets about
      <strong>1,787 kcal per day</strong>, with protein near 40 percent of that, about 179 grams.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Worked example 2: a man who wants to gain muscle</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      James is 27, 5 ft 11 in, and 180 lb, with a warehouse job and five lifting sessions a week. Converting: 180 lb is
      81.6 kg, and 71 inches is 180.3 cm. His BMR is (10 x 81.6) + (6.25 x 180.3) minus (5 x 27) + 5, which is 816 +
      1,127 minus 135 + 5, about <strong>1,813 kcal</strong>. A physical job plus five sessions puts him at very active,
      so his TDEE is 1,813 x 1.725, about <strong>3,128 kcal per day</strong>. For slow muscle gain he adds 250 and
      targets about <strong>3,378 kcal per day</strong>. Notice how much the activity factor matters: at sedentary his
      number would be only about 2,176.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-2xl font-extrabold text-ink-900">Step 4: Check your number against reality</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      A formula gives you an estimate, your scale gives you the verdict. Eat close to your TDEE for 2 to 3 weeks, weigh
      yourself on several mornings each week, and compare the weekly averages. If the average is flat, your hand
      calculation was right. If you are drifting up, your true TDEE is a little lower; drifting down means it is a little
      higher. Adjust by 100 to 200 kcal and repeat once, and you will know your personal TDEE more precisely than any
      formula or wearable can tell you. Then set your real goal with the
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/calculators/calorie-calculator/">Calorie Calculator</a> and split
      it into macros with the <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/calculators/macro-calculator/">Macro
      Calculator</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-8 rounded-xl bg-brand-50 border border-brand-100 px-5 py-4 text-sm text-ink-700">
      Reviewed by <strong>Waqar, Dietitian</strong>. Estimates only, not medical advice. See the
      <a class="font-bold text-brand-700 underline" href="/medical-disclaimer/">medical disclaimer</a>.
    </p>

    <Faq faqs={faqs} title="TDEE calculation questions" />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
